  ],
  "text": "A spacesuit is not clothing. It is a personal spacecraft with a person inside it. It holds pressure, supplies oxygen, removes carbon dioxide, and handles hundreds of degrees of swing. And it has to stop a micrometeorite moving faster than a bullet. So it is layers. A cooling garment threaded with water tubing sits against the skin. Then a pressure bladder, restraint layers to stop it ballooning, then armour, then insulation. But the real problem is that a pressurised suit does not want to bend at all. Inflate a glove and it becomes a stiff balloon that wants to stay open. So every time you close your hand you are fighting that pressure. All day. Hands tire within hours, and gloves are still the least solved part of the entire design. We can keep a person alive in a vacuum. We still cannot let them comfortably pick something up. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
